    "case_id": "719",
    "case_summary": "A 60-year-old female presented with initial symptoms 7 months ago including: dizziness, slight slant of mouth corners towards left, right-sided hearing drop, and diplopia. Symptoms worsened after 12 days with increased weakness of facial muscles. Past medical history includes hypertension for 5 years (treated with nifedipine 40mg/day irregularly) and obesity for 20 years without intervention.\n\n- Disappearance of nasolabial fold in right face- Left-slanted mouth corner- Incomplete right eyelid closure with visible sclera during maximal effort- No impairment of facial pain and temperature sensation- Weak muscle strength of right frontalis and orbicularis oculi- Unable to lift right eyebrow- No facial spasm or synkinesis- House-Brackmann grade V severity\n\n1. Brain MRI:- Lesion located in dorsolateral right pons- Hypo-intense signal in T1-weighted images- Moderate-intense signal in T2-weighted images- No hyper-intense signals in diffusion-weighted images2. MRTA (Magnetic resonance tomographic angiography):- No vertebrobasilar artery main trunk occlusion or stenosis3. Electrophysiological Tests:- ENoG: Significantly decreased amplitude of right facial nerve compound muscular action potential compared to healthy side- EMG: Presence of insertional activity in right facial muscles with greatly reduced voluntary activity",
